.TH FL-SECRETD 1 "November 2026" "fldesktop 0.1.4" "fldesktop"
.SH NAME
fl-secretd \- fldesktop's password store (the Secret Service)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfl-secretd\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBfl-secretd\fR keeps the passwords programs store through the Secret Service
API (\fBorg.freedesktop.secrets\fR on the session bus: libsecret, web browsers,
mail programs, NetworkManager's applets, Flatpak apps through the desktop
portal), in place of GNOME Keyring. \fBfl-keyring\fR(1) shows and edits them.
.PP
Passwords are kept in keyrings (collections), one file each in
\fI~/.local/share/fldesktop/keyrings\fR, encrypted with AES-256-GCM under a key
made from the keyring's password with scrypt. Labels, attributes and secrets
are all encrypted; a locked keyring only knows keyed hashes of the attributes,
so programs can still find their items and ask to unlock them. Secrets travel
to programs encrypted (the \fBdh-ietf1024-sha256-aes128-cbc-pkcs7\fR session
libsecret uses) or in plain form when a program asks for that.
.PP
The \fBlogin\fR keyring is the default one. With the PAM module it is made
with, and unlocked by, the login password; a password change re-encrypts it.
Without the module (automatic login), the first program that needs it shows
an unlock dialog. Other keyrings ask for their own password. The
\fBsession\fR keyring is kept in memory only.
.PP
\fBfl-session\fR starts it; D-Bus starts it too when a program asks first. If
GNOME Keyring or KeePassXC already serves \fBorg.freedesktop.secrets\fR,
\fBfl-secretd\fR leaves it to them and exits.
.SH PAM
\fBpam_fldesktop_keyring.so\fR, in the auth, session (after
\fBpam_systemd\fR) and password stacks, for example in
\fI/etc/pam.d/greetd\fR:
.PP
.nf
auth      optional  pam_fldesktop_keyring.so
session   optional  pam_fldesktop_keyring.so
password  optional  pam_fldesktop_keyring.so
.fi
.PP
On Debian, \fBpam-auth-update\fR adds it to the common stacks. The module
never makes a login fail.
.SH FILES
\fI~/.local/share/fldesktop/keyrings/*.keyring\fR, \fIaliases.ini\fR
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBfl-keyring\fR(1), \fBsecret-tool\fR(1), \fBpinentry-fldesktop\fR(1)
